</style>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/font-awesome/4.7.0/css/font-awesome.min.css">
</head>
<body class="article toc2 toc-right">
<div id="header">
<h1>Headless API</h1>
<div id="toc" class="toc2">
<div id="toctitle">Table of Contents</div>
<ul class="sectlevel1">
<li><a href="#guillotine">Guillotine</a></li>
<li><a href="#query_playground">Query Playground</a>
<ul class="sectlevel2">
<li><a href="#task_query_your_artists">Task: Query your artists</a></li>
<li><a href="#getting_around">Getting around</a></li>
<li><a href="#task_explore_the_graphql_types">Task: Explore the GraphQL types</a></li>
<li><a href="#task_select_the_artist_fields">Task: Select the Artist fields</a></li>
<li><a href="#task_retrieve_one_artist">Task: Retrieve one artist</a></li>
<li><a href="#task_inspect_the_form_data">Task: Inspect the form data</a></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><a href="#search_your_content">Search your content</a>
<ul class="sectlevel2">
<li><a href="#task_find_artists_by_their_description">Task: Find artists by their description</a></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><a href="#extend_the_content_model">Extend the content model</a>
<ul class="sectlevel2">
<li><a href="#task_add_and_query_birthdays">Task: Add and query birthdays</a></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><a href="#other_clients">Other clients</a></li>
<li><a href="#dive_deeper">Dive deeper</a></li>
<li><a href="#coming_up">Coming up</a></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="content">
<div id="preamble">
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>In this chapter, you&#8217;ll query your artists through Guillotine, Enonic&#8217;s GraphQL API, and see how changes to your content model appear in the API.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="guillotine"><a class="link" href="#guillotine">Guillotine</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Guillotine generates a GraphQL API from your content types, letting you query both common content fields and the fields you define in YAML.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>It was installed with the <code>Essentials</code> sandbox template.
You&#8217;ll explore it through Content Studio&#8217;s Query Playground.</p>
</div>
<div class="admonitionblock note">
<table>
<tr>
<td class="icon">
<i class="fa icon-note" title="Note"></i>
</td>
<td class="content">
This tutorial is written for Guillotine 9, which requires XP 8.1 or newer. Check the version in <code>XP menu</code> &#8594; <code>Applications</code> if your queries do not behave as described.
</td>
</tr>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="query_playground"><a class="link" href="#query_playground">Query Playground</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Back in Content Studio, click the GraphQL icon in the menu bar on the left to open Query Playground:</p>
</div>
<div class="imageblock">
<div class="content">
<img src="media/api-playground.png" alt="Query Playground in Content Studio, with the query editor on the left, response panel on the right, and Draft selected" width="1440">
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Query Playground is an interactive GraphQL API explorer. You enter your query in the editor in the middle and when you execute it, you&#8217;ll get the results of the query in the panel on the right.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>GraphQL lets you request exactly the fields you need from an API.
The <a href="https://graphql.org/learn/">official GraphQL documentation</a> explains the query language in more detail.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Select your "My First Project" project and the <strong>Draft</strong> branch in Query Playground.
Keep Draft selected throughout this chapter so your queries include the changes you make in Content Studio.</p>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="task_query_your_artists"><a class="link" href="#task_query_your_artists">Task: Query your artists</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Use <code>queryDsl</code> to find content by its type.
Run this query to retrieve up to ten artists, sorted by display name:</p>
</div>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-GraphQL" data-lang="GraphQL">{
  guillotine {
    queryDsl(
      query: {
        term: {
          field: "type"
          value: { string: "com.example.dev101:artist" }
        }
      }
      first: 10
      sort: [{ field: "displayName", direction: ASC }]
    ) {
      displayName
      _path
      _id
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The <code>term</code> matches the full content type name, including your app&#8217;s namespace.
The Artists folder keeps your content organized, but this query finds artists anywhere in the selected content project.
<code>first</code> limits the number of results, and <code>sort</code> puts them in alphabetical order.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>You should get Cardi B, Missy Elliott and P!nk in the response panel, in that order.
The response has this structure, with the results in the <code>data.guillotine.queryDsl</code> array.
Your content IDs will differ from these example values, and the paths depend on the names you gave your items:</p>
</div>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="title">Example response</div>
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-json" data-lang="json">{
  "data": {
    "guillotine": {
      "queryDsl": [
        {
          "displayName": "Cardi B",
          "_path": "/artists/cardi-b",
          "_id": "a0d019c7-b37a-4eaa-a2d0-96a005dc33ff"
        },
        {
          "displayName": "Missy Elliott",
          "_path": "/artists/missy-elliott",
          "_id": "3caace62-9527-4174-8024-939a91945a07"
        },
        {
          "displayName": "P!nk",
          "_path": "/artists/pnk",
          "_id": "e079cf41-527a-474f-8a9f-89a1826f14d8"
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Try changing <code>first</code> to <code>1</code> and <code>direction</code> to <code>DESC</code>, then run the query again.
You should get only P!nk, the last artist alphabetically.
Restore <code>first: 10</code> and <code>direction: ASC</code> before continuing.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="getting_around"><a class="link" href="#getting_around">Getting around</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Before trying to come up with complex queries, let&#8217;s familiarize ourselves with Query Playground. It consists of a few panels:</p>
</div>
<div class="dlist">
<dl>
<dt class="hdlist1">Docs</dt>
<dd>
<p>Opens on the left, and lets you browse every type and field in the API. We&#8217;ll use it in a moment.</p>
</dd>
<dt class="hdlist1">Query</dt>
<dd>
<p>The editor in the middle, where you write your query. Press the play button, or <span class="keyseq"><kbd>ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>enter</kbd></span>, to run it.</p>
</dd>
<dt class="hdlist1">Response</dt>
<dd>
<p>The panel on the right, showing the result of the query, or any errors.</p>
</dd>
<dt class="hdlist1">Variables</dt>
<dd>
<p>Expands below the query editor, and lets you supply query variables as a JSON object.</p>
</dd>
<dt class="hdlist1">Draft / Master</dt>
<dd>
<p>The buttons above the response panel select which branch to query. Draft is your work in progress, Master is what has been published.</p>
</dd>
</dl>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Queries run in the project currently selected in Content Studio&#8217;s top bar, so you never have to tell Query Playground which project to use.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="task_explore_the_graphql_types"><a class="link" href="#task_explore_the_graphql_types">Task: Explore the GraphQL types</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The YAML content type schema you wrote defines the Artist form and its data in the CMS.
Guillotine uses that definition to generate GraphQL types for querying your artists.
These types are part of the <em>GraphQL schema</em>: the API&#8217;s description of the types, fields and operations available to query.
The <a href="https://graphql.org/learn/schema/">GraphQL documentation</a> explains schemas and types in more detail.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Open the Docs panel to explore those types.
Search for <code>artist</code> and locate the <code>{app-name-query}_Artist</code> object type:</p>
</div>
<div class="imageblock">
<div class="content">
<img src="media/api-doc.png" alt="Query Playground Docs panel showing the Artist GraphQL object type, its Content interface, and common fields" width="477">
</div>
<div class="title">Figure 1. The Artist GraphQL type</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Notice that the Artist object type <em>implements</em> the <code>Content</code> interface.
An interface defines fields shared by the object types that implement it.
Here, <code>Content</code> defines common content fields such as <code>displayName</code>, <code>_path</code> and <code>_id</code>, so you can request them for any content item.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The Artist object type also has a <code>data</code> field whose type is <code>{app-name-query}_Artist_Data</code>.
Follow that type in the Docs panel and find <code>legalName</code> and <code>about</code>.
These are the fields you defined in your YAML form.</p>
</div>
<div class="admonitionblock note">
<table>
<tr>
<td class="icon">
<i class="fa icon-note" title="Note"></i>
</td>
<td class="content">
Your CMS content type is named <code>com.example.dev101:artist</code>, and its generated GraphQL object type is <code>{app-name-query}_Artist</code>.
The app namespace carries over to the API, keeping the types from different apps apart.
</td>
</tr>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="task_select_the_artist_fields"><a class="link" href="#task_select_the_artist_fields">Task: Select the Artist fields</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>You&#8217;ve inspected the Artist GraphQL type in the Docs panel.
Now you&#8217;ll query the fields from your content type&#8217;s form.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Close the Docs panel, head back to the editor and remove <code>displayName</code>, <code>_path</code> and <code>_id</code> from the query we introduced earlier.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Now, put your cursor inside the result selection braces immediately after <code>queryDsl(&#8230;&#8203;)</code>, and press <span class="keyseq"><kbd>ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>space</kbd></span> to see which fields you can request.
Look for the common fields <code>displayName</code>, <code>_path</code> and <code>_id</code> in the suggestions.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The artist&#8217;s <code>legalName</code> and <code>about</code> fields are not available directly here.
<code>queryDsl</code> returns the general <code>Content</code> interface, even when its filter selects only artists.
The filter controls which items match; it does not change the query field&#8217;s declared GraphQL return type.
To select the Artist type&#8217;s own fields, use an <a href="https://graphql.org/learn/queries/#inline-fragments">inline fragment</a>.
The syntax <code>... on {app-name-query}_Artist { ... }</code> means "for results whose GraphQL type is Artist, select the fields inside this block."
This gives you access to the Artist&#8217;s <code>data</code> fields:</p>
</div>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-GraphQL" data-lang="GraphQL">{
  guillotine {
    queryDsl(
      query: {
        term: {
          field: "type"
          value: { string: "com.example.dev101:artist" }
        }
      }
      first: 10
      sort: [{ field: "displayName", direction: ASC }]
    ) {
      ... on com_example_dev101_Artist {
        _id
        data {
          legalName
          about
        }
      }
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The filter selects artists, and the inline fragment lets you request their <code>legalName</code> and <code>about</code> fields under <code>data</code>, along with the general content field <code>_id</code>.
Below is one example item from the <code>data.guillotine.queryDsl</code> array.
The field values depend on what you entered in Content Studio:</p>
</div>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="title">One artist result</div>
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-json" data-lang="json">{
  "_id": "a0d019c7-b37a-4eaa-a2d0-96a005dc33ff",
  "data": {
    "legalName": "Belcalis Marlenis Almánzar",
    "about": "Belcalis Marlenis Almánzar (born October 11, 1992), known professionally as Cardi B, is an American rapper and songwriter."
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="task_retrieve_one_artist"><a class="link" href="#task_retrieve_one_artist">Task: Retrieve one artist</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>When you know an item&#8217;s path or ID, use <code>get</code> to retrieve it directly.
Copy Cardi B&#8217;s <code>_path</code> from the first response and use it as the <code>key</code> below:</p>
</div>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-GraphQL" data-lang="GraphQL">{
  guillotine {
    get(key: "/artists/cardi-b") {
      displayName
      ... on com_example_dev101_Artist {
        data {
          legalName
          about
        }
      }
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The result is one object under <code>data.guillotine.get</code>, rather than an array of results.
Like <code>queryDsl</code>, <code>get</code> returns the <code>Content</code> interface, so you use an inline fragment to select the Artist fields.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Try replacing the path with Cardi B&#8217;s <code>_id</code> from your first response.
Both keys retrieve the same item.
An ID stays the same when an item is renamed or moved; its path changes.
If the key does not match an item in the selected project and branch, <code>get</code> returns <code>null</code>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="task_inspect_the_form_data"><a class="link" href="#task_inspect_the_form_data">Task: Inspect the form data</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>For a quick look at all stored form data, use <code>dataAsJson</code>.
Update the single-artist query to:</p>
</div>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-GraphQL" data-lang="GraphQL">{
  guillotine {
    get(key: "/artists/cardi-b") {
      displayName
      dataAsJson
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>This returns all stored form fields without selecting them individually.
At this point, your Artist form has only <code>legalName</code> and <code>about</code>.
The object under <code>data.guillotine.get</code> looks like this, with the values you entered in Content Studio:</p>
</div>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="title">Cardi B&#8217;s form data</div>
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-json" data-lang="json">{
  "displayName": "Cardi B",
  "dataAsJson": {
    "legalName": "Belcalis Marlenis Almánzar",
    "about": "Belcalis Marlenis Almánzar (born October 11, 1992), known professionally as Cardi B, is an American rapper and songwriter."
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Use <code>dataAsJson</code> to inspect stored values.
Use typed fields when you want to select specific fields or follow references, which you&#8217;ll try in a later chapter.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="search_your_content"><a class="link" href="#search_your_content">Search your content</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The <code>queryDsl</code> field also supports text searches.
The "Dsl" stands for "Domain Specific Language": the structured query you pass to it describes which content to find.</p>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="task_find_artists_by_their_description"><a class="link" href="#task_find_artists_by_their_description">Task: Find artists by their description</a></h3>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Keep the Artist type filter and add a text search on <code>data.about</code>:</p>
</div>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-GraphQL" data-lang="GraphQL">{
  guillotine {
    queryDsl(
      query: {
        boolean: {
          must: [
            {
              term: {
                field: "type"
                value: { string: "com.example.dev101:artist" }
              }
            }
            {
              ngram: {
                fields: ["data.about"]
                query: "rap"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      }
      first: 10
      sort: [{ field: "displayName", direction: ASC }]
    ) {
      displayName
      ... on com_example_dev101_Artist {
        data {
          about
        }
      }
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The <code>boolean.must</code> list requires both conditions to match: the item must be an Artist, and its <code>about</code> text must match <code>rap</code>.
The <code>ngram</code> expression finds partial words, so it can match "rapper".
With the example descriptions, you should get Cardi B and Missy Elliott.
The query returns each description so you can see why it matched.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>If your descriptions use different words, change <code>rap</code> to part of a word you entered, or edit a description in Content Studio, save it and run the query again.
The <a href="https://developer.enonic.com/docs/code/stable/storage/querying">Query DSL reference</a> covers more search expressions and combinations.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="extend_the_content_model"><a class="link" href="#extend_the_content_model">Extend the content model</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Adding a form field also makes it available through GraphQL.
You&#8217;ll add a birthday to the Artist content type and read it back through the API.</p>
</div>
<div class="sect2">
<h3 id="task_add_and_query_birthdays"><a class="link" href="#task_add_and_query_birthdays">Task: Add and query birthdays</a></h3>
<div class="olist arabic">
<ol class="arabic">
<li>
<p>Update the Artist content type:</p>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="title">src/main/resources/cms/content-types/artist/artist.yaml</div>
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-yaml" data-lang="yaml">kind: "ContentType"
title: "Artist"
description: "Information about an artist"
displayNamePlaceholder: "Stage name"
superType: "base:structured"
form:
  - type: "TextLine"
    name: "legalName"
    label: "Legal name"
    helpText: "The artist's name on official documents, if different from their stage name."
  - type: "TextArea"
    name: "about"
    label: "About the artist"
  - type: "Date"
    name: "birthday"
    label: "Birthday"</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The new <code>birthday</code> field uses the Date form item.
Save the file while <code>enonic dev</code> is running, as in the previous chapter, and wait for the app to redeploy.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<p>Open each artist in Content Studio, enter the birthday below and save:</p>
<div class="ulist">
<ul>
<li>
<p>Cardi B: <code>1992-10-11</code></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Missy Elliott: <code>1971-07-01</code></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>P!nk: <code>1979-09-08</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<p>Close and reopen Query Playground to reload the GraphQL schema.
Keep the <strong>Draft</strong> branch selected: these edits have not been published.
Find <code>{app-name-query}_Artist_Data</code> in the Docs panel and notice its new <code>birthday</code> field.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Run the artist query with <code>birthday</code> selected:</p>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-GraphQL" data-lang="GraphQL">{
  guillotine {
    queryDsl(
      query: {
        term: {
          field: "type"
          value: { string: "com.example.dev101:artist" }
        }
      }
      first: 10
      sort: [{ field: "displayName", direction: ASC }]
    ) {
      displayName
      ... on com_example_dev101_Artist {
        data {
          birthday
        }
      }
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The <code>data.guillotine.queryDsl</code> array now contains:</p>
</div>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="title">Artists with birthdays</div>
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-json" data-lang="json">[
  {
    "displayName": "Cardi B",
    "data": {
      "birthday": "1992-10-11"
    }
  },
  {
    "displayName": "Missy Elliott",
    "data": {
      "birthday": "1971-07-01"
    }
  },
  {
    "displayName": "P!nk",
    "data": {
      "birthday": "1979-09-08"
    }
  }
]</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>You changed the CMS schema and entered content; Guillotine made the new field available without any API code.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="other_clients"><a class="link" href="#other_clients">Other clients</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>From outside Content Studio, send these same queries to <code><a href="http://localhost:8080/api/com.enonic.app.guillotine:graphql" class="bare">http://localhost:8080/api/com.enonic.app.guillotine:graphql</a></code>.
This endpoint is shared by all content projects.
An external client must specify the content project and branch, and authenticate to read drafts; Query Playground supplies that context for you.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>For example, the single-artist query with explicit project and branch arguments looks like this:</p>
</div>
<div class="listingblock">
<div class="content">
<pre class="highlight"><code class="language-GraphQL" data-lang="GraphQL">{
  guillotine(project: "my-first-project", branch: "draft") {
    get(key: "/artists/cardi-b") {
      displayName
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>The <code>project</code> argument is the content project&#8217;s identifier, <code>my-first-project</code>, rather than its display name, "My First Project".
Setting <code>branch: "draft"</code> selects unpublished work; it does not authenticate the request.
Use <code>branch: "master"</code> to read published content.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>Guillotine is a <em>Universal API</em>, served at <code>/api/&lt;app&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code>.
Its API name, <code>com.enonic.app.guillotine:graphql</code>, follows the API base URL, <code><a href="http://localhost:8080/api" class="bare">http://localhost:8080/api</a></code> on your sandbox.
The media APIs you&#8217;ll use in the <a href="files.ahtml">next chapter</a> follow the same pattern.</p>
</div>
<div class="paragraph">
<p>For browser requests from another origin, the server must allow that origin through CORS.
The Guillotine documentation covers <a href="https://developer.enonic.com/docs/guillotine/stable/usage#curl">cURL requests</a>, <a href="https://developer.enonic.com/docs/guillotine/stable/usage#third-party-clients">third-party GraphQL clients</a> and <a href="https://developer.enonic.com/docs/guillotine/stable/configuration#config_file">CORS configuration for browsers</a>.
You&#8217;ll fetch your published artists from code in the <a href="frontend.ahtml">front-end chapter</a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="dive_deeper"><a class="link" href="#dive_deeper">Dive deeper</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="ulist">
<ul>
<li>
<p>For more query examples and information about how to get up and running with Guillotine, check out the <a href="https://developer.enonic.com/docs/guillotine">Guillotine docs</a></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>For pagination and aggregations, see the <a href="https://developer.enonic.com/docs/guillotine/stable/api#querydslcontentconnection">queryDslConnection reference</a>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>If you want to learn more about GraphQL in general, the <a href="https://graphql.org/learn/"><em>Introduction to GraphQL</em> documentation</a> is a good place to start</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="sect1">
<h2 id="coming_up"><a class="link" href="#coming_up">Coming up</a></h2>
<div class="sectionbody">
<div class="paragraph">
<p>You&#8217;ve found artists by type, selected their fields, retrieved a single item, searched descriptions and queried a new field from your content model.
In the next chapter, you&#8217;ll see <a href="files.ahtml">how XP deals with files and images</a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div id="footer">
<div id="footer-text">
Last updated 2026-10-27 11:08:34 UTC
</div>
</div>
</body>
